      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Real-world impact</p>
      <h2 class="section-title mb-12">Sector scenarios & case studies</h2>
      <div class="reveal grid-2">
        <div class="prose-content">
          <p>A regional energy utility in the Gulf operated a vast SCADA network controlling power generation, substation switching, and load distribution across thousands of feeders. Decades of incremental expansion had left operational technology systems connected to corporate IT networks with minimal segmentation, exposing critical control systems to lateral movement from compromised workstations. MEAICON deployed a Purdue-model segmentation architecture that isolated control-system traffic from enterprise networks, installed OT-aware intrusion detection that understood Modbus and IEC 61850 protocol baselines, and established a dedicated OT security operations centre with twenty-four-hour monitoring. The utility eliminated unauthorized cross-zone traffic within weeks and gained real-time visibility into anomalous control-system activity.</p>
          <p>In North Africa, a national water utility faced intermittent disruptions to remote pumping stations and treatment plants. Field investigation revealed that unauthenticated remote-access connections used by maintenance contractors were being exploited to modify setpoints and disable alarms. MEAICON implemented identity-based remote access with just-in-time credentialing, session recording, and automatic revocation after maintenance windows closed. All remote engineering sessions now pass through a hardened bastion architecture with full audit logging, satisfying the utility's regulator and restoring confidence in remote operations.</p>
          <p>Across East Africa, a regional power distributor consolidating grid operations needed to converge multiple legacy control systems onto a unified monitoring platform without interrupting live operations. MEAICON architected a phased data-collection layer that ingested telemetry from diverse protocol environments, normalized it into a common operational picture, and fed both the operations team's dashboards and the security monitoring pipeline. This convergence reduced mean-time-to-detect for grid anomalies and provided the security team with context-rich alerts tied to physical infrastructure events.</p>
        </div>
